clearcote: report the D3D11 shader-precision profile for a Windows persona on a non-D3D host.

D3D11 has no reduced float precision -- HLSL SM4+ promotes every tier to highp -- so a real
Direct3D11 renderer answers [127,127,23] for float and [31,30,0] for int at EVERY tier and BOTH
stages. Measured on genuine Chrome 151, real Windows, RTX 3070: all twelve queries return exactly
those two values, with no per-tier variation at all.

ANGLE's Vulkan/SwiftShader backend instead reports true fp16 for mediump/lowp ([15,15],10), which
sits directly beside a renderer string naming Direct3D11. Reading it needs no reference data and no
population baseline: the renderer names the API, and the precision profile contradicts it.

Sits in the same switch as the existing mobile branch, which spoofs the opposite direction, and
returns before the real GetShaderPrecisionFormat() call -- ClampPersonaLimit is not on this path, so
no clamp exception is involved.

SAFE because precision formats are ADVISORY. SwiftShader computes at 32-bit float regardless, so no
shader that compiled before stops compiling and no allocation is backed by these numbers. That is
exactly what distinguishes this from an upward limit spoof, which the backend would have to honour.

Gated on IS_LINUX plus an explicit Windows persona: a real-GPU Vulkan Linux host honours
RelaxedPrecision and keeps its honest answer, a Windows host is already correct, and an absent
--fingerprint-platform falls back to the host build (the IsWindowsPersona convention).

--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgl/webgl_rendering_context_base.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgl/webgl_rendering_context_base.cc
@@ -5023,6 +5023,39 @@
     return MakeGarbageCollected<WebGLShaderPrecisionFormat>(r0, r1, p);
   }
 
+  // clearcote(r16): a Windows persona on a non-D3D host reports the D3D11 precision
+  // profile. D3D11 has no reduced float precision -- HLSL SM4+ promotes every tier to
+  // highp -- so a real Direct3D11 renderer answers [127,127,23] for float and [31,30,0]
+  // for int at EVERY tier and BOTH stages. Measured on genuine Chrome 151, real Windows,
+  // RTX 3070: all twelve queries return exactly those two values. ANGLE's Vulkan backend
+  // instead reports true fp16 mediump/lowp ([15,15],10), which sits directly beside a
+  // renderer string naming Direct3D11 and needs no reference data to read.
+  //
+  // Gated on IS_LINUX (compile time) plus a Windows persona: a real-GPU Vulkan Linux host
+  // honours RelaxedPrecision and must keep its honest answer, and a Windows host is
+  // already correct. Advisory metadata only -- SwiftShader computes at 32-bit float
+  // regardless, so no shader that compiled before stops compiling. This is what
+  // distinguishes it from an upward limit spoof, which Dawn/Blink would have to back.
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)
+  if (!ungoogled::CurrentPersona().webgl_real &&
+      ungoogled::GetPersonaPlatform() == ungoogled::PersonaPlatform::kWindows) {
+    GLint r0 = 127, r1 = 127, p = 23;
+    switch (precision_type) {
+      case GL_HIGH_FLOAT:
+      case GL_MEDIUM_FLOAT:
+      case GL_LOW_FLOAT:
+        r0 = 127; r1 = 127; p = 23; break;
+      case GL_HIGH_INT:
+      case GL_MEDIUM_INT:
+      case GL_LOW_INT:
+        r0 = 31; r1 = 30; p = 0; break;
+      default:
+        break;
+    }
+    return MakeGarbageCollected<WebGLShaderPrecisionFormat>(r0, r1, p);
+  }
+#endif
+
   GLint range[2] = {0, 0};
   GLint precision = 0;
   ContextGL()->GetShaderPrecisionFormat(shader_type, precision_type, range,
